there's a battle going on in america's  skies right now over the future of  spirit airlines see on one side fellow  ultra low cost carrier frontier airlines  is trying to merge with spirit and on  the other slightly higher budget jetblue  is attempting a hostile takeover of  spirit now this battle has the potential  to reshape america's aviation sector so  to win both sides have been locked in a  war of words as they try and convince  spirit and its shareholders that they'd  be a better match than their rival so  how did we get here why is spirit so  coveted and what could this mean for  passengers let's dive in  [Music]  okay so here's how we got here after a  deal had been rumored for years in early  february of 2022 frontier offered to buy  spirit for 2.9 billion dollars in cash  and stock the deal was set to close by  the end of this year but then in comes  jet blue with a rival unsolicited offer  so they come in with a 3.6 billion  dollar offer i'll cash that's wsj's  alison cider who has been following this  battle closely now spirit has a little  bit of a dilemma they have you know this  partner that they've been talking to for  months and months and they're excited  about the deal and now jetblue is  offering you know what at least jeff  blue describes as an offer that's too  good to refuse despite it being higher  spirit rejects jetblue's offer over  worries around antitrust concerns and  jetblue obviously was unhappy with that  so jetblue begins a hostile takeover  attempt by appealing to spirit  shareholders offering to buy their  shares for 30 each and encouraging them  to vote against the frontier deal  basically saying you know spirit  shareholders this will send a message to  spirits board and management that you  really don't want to do this frontier  deal that this jet blue deal is much  better for you so it's sort of a long  saga but that's where we are  so why is there such an intense battle  over a no frills airline well whoever  takes spirit would become the fifth  biggest airline in america in terms of  seat capacity and both jetblue and  frontier have said that would put them  in a better position to take on the big  four of delta united south west and  american airlines they just think spirit  is sort of like a once in a lifetime  opportunity to grow more quickly than  they can grow on their own now frontier  has a similar business model and similar  planes to spirit but they mainly operate  on different sides of america they're  arguing sort of that they're putting  these networks together it's really  going to make them a national presence  and for jetblue taking spirit could help  them avoid pilot shortages and backlogs  for new plane orders it's going to take  spirits big yellow planes with the seats  all smushed really close together it's  gonna rip out some rows make a little  more legroom you know retrofit them so  they look just like jetblue planes but  there could be another reason why these  two companies so desperately want spirit  and that's to stop the other side from  getting it some analysts have suggested  like if spirit and frontier merge  suddenly they're the fifth largest  carrier blue's getting bumps lower on  the list maybe that leaves them a little  vulnerable what do they do next so what  could a potential deal mean for you the  passenger well it could affect ticket  prices root choices and seat  availability here's how first off ticket  prices now jetblue and frontier both  argue that the presence of a bigger  low-cost carrier would force the big  four to have to lower their ticket  prices and expand their own economy  offerings to remain competitive but a  deal would also mean there's one less  budget carrier operating in america and  less choice often means higher prices  next is roots because to avoid falling  foul of anti-trust regulators jetblue  and frontier could have to sacrifice  some airport slots gates and routes  jetblue has said like they preemptively  agreed to make